en:
Suppose the energy of a field is smallest at one value and rises when the field is displaced. If the field is disturbed, it oscillates about the minimum. Quantum mechanics quantizes those oscillations, and the individual quanta are particles. This is the field-theory dictionary needed throughout the lecture: $\text{particle}
\quad\longleftrightarrow\quad
\text{quantum of a field vibration}.$

ja:
場のエネルギーが一つの値で最小になり、場が変位すると増大するとしよう。場が乱されれば、それは最小値のまわりで振動する。量子力学はそれらの振動を量子化し、個々の量子は粒子である。これが講義全体で必要となる場の理論の辞書である。 $\text{particle}
\quad\longleftrightarrow\quad
\text{quantum of a field vibration}.$

zh:
假设一个场的能量在某个取值处最小，而当该场偏离该取值时能量上升。如果该场受到扰动，它就会围绕最小值振荡。量子力学将这些振荡量子化，而单个量子就是粒子。这就是整场讲座都需要的场论词典： $\text{particle}
\quad\longleftrightarrow\quad
\text{quantum of a field vibration}.$

p.src:
Classically, one might put the field at a point on the rim, set its angular velocity to zero, and declare the problem finished. Quantum mechanics forbids so sharp a conclusion. Position and momentum obey $\Delta x\,\Delta p\gtrsim\frac{\hbar}{2}.$ The analogous statement on the circular trough is that localizing the field’s angle makes its conjugate internal angular momentum uncertain. In the lecture’s schematic language, $\Delta\theta\,\Delta L_{\mathrm{int}}
\gtrsim
\frac{\hbar}{2}.$  (Editorial clarification: Angle operators have technical subtleties, so this relation is a local semiclassical expression of the lecture’s uncertainty argument rather than a globally exact operator identity.)

