p.src:
The natural upper benchmark for elementary-particle masses is the Planck mass. Push a localized energy far enough and gravity can no longer be neglected; eventually the object is better regarded as a black hole than as an ordinary elementary particle. Numerically, $m_{\mathrm P}
=
\sqrt{\frac{\hbar c}{G}}
\approx
2.2\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{kg},$ roughly the mass of a tiny dust mote. Known elementary particles are fantastically lighter. In the scale estimate used in the lecture, their masses reach only about $10^{-17}$ of the Planck mass.

